  "response": "The methodological lesson drawn from both designs is the same: credible adjudication requires some source of variation in neighborhood exposure that is unrelated to the unmeasured attributes of parents, whether that variation comes from a lottery or from the differing ages of children within a single relocating household. Dose-response patterns in age at exposure now carry most of the evidentiary weight in the field, and descriptive comparisons across neighborhoods are treated as upper bounds rather than as estimates of place effects.\n\nA second line of work rests on administrative data rather than on an experiment. Chetty and Hendren analyzed more than five million families who moved between commuting zones, and the earnings trajectories of their children indicate that each additional year of childhood spent in a higher-opportunity area raises a child's later earnings. In other words, the advantage of moving to a better neighborhood accumulates in proportion to the number of childhood years spent there. Because the gradient is estimated by comparing siblings who were different ages at the time of the same family move, its interpretation does not depend on contrasts between families who move and families who never do.\n\nRaj Chetty, Nathaniel Hendren, and Lawrence Katz linked Moving to Opportunity children to adult tax records, a procedure that allowed earnings to be measured decades after the vouchers were issued. Either the experiment produced no detectable adult earnings gains for any group of children, or it produced detectable adult earnings gains for children who moved before age thirteen; exactly one of these descriptions can be correct, and the other must fail. The tax-record linkage establishes the second of them, showing that the experiment produced detectable adult earnings gains for children who moved before age thirteen. Chetty and colleagues found that earnings gains were concentrated among children who moved after age sixteen, a conclusion that the gains recorded for those who moved before thirteen rule out.\n\nThe competing accounts can be stated sharply enough to be tested. At least one of the following must hold, and perhaps both: families living in high-poverty neighborhoods have characteristics that independently depress their children's mobility, or residence in a high-poverty neighborhood causally reduces children's adult earnings. The entire task of the evidence is to apportion weight between these two possibilities, since the raw correlation between neighborhood poverty and downward mobility is equally consistent with either of them doing most of the work.\n\nAdjudication in this literature turns on a single inferential problem. Observational estimates of neighborhood effects are vulnerable to omitted-variable bias arising from residential selection, and it was precisely this defect in the observational record that prompted the field to mount randomized housing mobility designs. The Moving to Opportunity demonstration randomly assigned housing vouchers to families living in high-poverty public housing, and that randomization thereby broke the statistical link between family characteristics and neighborhood of residence. The demonstration randomized families in twelve metropolitan areas, which is why its results are read as speaking to urban contexts in general rather than to the peculiarities of a single city.",
